#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""spike — assemble a hermetic single-file HTML build from a body fragment, and screenshot it.

The point of this tool is that the stylesheets and harness JS never enter the model's
context: it writes content, the tool supplies everything else. See
~/.claude/skills/spike/CONTRACT.md for the fragment contract.

Three fidelities of the same question — what should this look like:

  wireframe  greybox structure, colour withheld on purpose
  mockup     static HTML in the project's real CSS: variants x states, no harness chrome,
             screenshotted by `spike shot`
  prototype  working versions of one UI behind the Tweaks panel, in the project's own tokens

A TERMINAL screen is `spike tui`, not a --kind: its output is a Go program, because 80
columns, 16 theme-assigned colours and a keyboard the design owns are the whole question
and none of them exist in an HTML mock. See ~/.claude/skills/spike/TUI.md.

Explainers are a separate kind of document with their own tool, `explainer`.

  spike build --kind wireframe|mockup|prototype --title <t>
              --fragment <abs.html> --out <abs.html>
              [--subtitle <s>] [--lang en] [--extra-css <abs.css>]
  spike shot  <slug> [--dir <abs-dir>] [--size WxH] [--scale N]
              [--theme light|dark|both] [--cell id,id] [--query q]
  spike video  <slug> [--dir <abs-dir>] [--size WxH] [--scale N] [--theme light|dark|both]
              [--cell id,id] [--query q] [--reduced] [--fps N] [--duration ms] [--hold ms]
              [--strip N] [--quality Q]
  spike tui   --out <abs-dir> --title <t> [--force] [--force-gomod]
  spike kinds
  spike serve <abs-path> [--port 8000]
"""

